<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.11.4: Identifying the Features of a Graph</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00247</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.11.4: Identifying the Features of a Graph</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4748f5b1-509c-4960-accb-50e70d3bce6a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">For numbers 1 – 3, consider the equation <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>1.5</m:mn><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4.5</m:mn><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>18</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00002">If we graph the equation, what is the slope of the graph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00003">Compare your answer: </para>
          <para id="para-00004">The slope is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>. Rearranging the equation to isolate <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mtext> </m:mtext><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mtext> </m:mtext><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00005">Where does the graph intersect the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">The graph intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> in <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>+</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math> tells us where the graph crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00007">Where does it intersect the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00008">The graph intersects the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>. Substituting 0 for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> in the original equation and solving for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> gives <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
